# Elgiganten Tracking Portal - Cursor Guidelines (ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE)
#
# NOTE: This file is written by an external customer without access to the
# actual codebase. Status names, module boundaries and conventions below are
# placeholders and must be mapped to Elgiganten's real domain model and to
# Bring's actual API status codes before use.

## Project Context
- Domain: Customer-facing tracking portal and internal support dashboard.
- Assumed stack: TypeScript, Node.js, Playwright (E2E testing).
- Primary carrier API: Bring Logistics.

## Core Business Rules

1. The "No Phantom Delivery" rule
   The tracking UI must never display a specific delivery date or time window
   (e.g. "09:00-15:00") unless the carrier API has confirmed physical custody
   of the parcel — i.e. a real scan event, not an internal forecast.
   Map to the actual Bring status codes for "received at terminal" and
   "out for delivery".

2. Single source of truth
   If the internal warehouse status says DISPATCHED but the carrier API reports
   the parcel as not received, the UI must fall back to the carrier's truth
   ("Awaiting carrier pickup" / "Väntar på inlämning till transportör").
   Internal optimism must never override carrier reality.

3. Distinguish forecast from fact
   A predicted delivery date is legitimate, but must be labelled as an estimate
   ("Beräknad leverans") and must never be rendered in the same component or
   visual style as a confirmed time window ("Levereras 09:00-15:00").

4. Support dashboard parity
   Data shown to the customer on the tracking page must match what Level 1
   support agents see. Support views should read live carrier data rather than
   cached internal warehouse status, so agents cannot confirm a delivery that
   the carrier has no record of.

5. Fail loud, not silent
   If the carrier API is unreachable or returns an unknown status, show an
   explicit "status unavailable" state. Never fall back to internal estimates
   presented as confirmed facts.

## Testing Guidelines
- E2E tests must mock the carrier API to cover edge cases: parcel never
  received, parcel delayed, API timeout, unknown status code.
- Assert both directions: the time window must be hidden when carrier data is
  missing, AND shown when the carrier has confirmed custody.
- Never point automated tests at production URLs; use a configurable base URL.
